1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,100
This is a weird one.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,100
What if our entire universe is inside a black hole?

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,460
I mean that literally.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,420
What if everything we can see,

5
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,020
from the galaxy to the star, planet,

6
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:12,920
even you sitting there watching this,

7
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,800
is actually on the inside of a black hole

8
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,060
that exists in some much larger universe?

9
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,200
Now, I know what you're probably thinking.

10
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,500
How's that even possible?

11
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:24,940
Because when we think about a black hole,

12
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,620
we usually picture this giant dark object

13
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:32,380
somewhere out in space, pulling everything toward it.

14
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,440
But that's not really what a black hole is.

15
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:36,100
And once you understand that,

16
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,640
this whole idea starts getting a lot more interesting.

17
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:42,400
You see, a black hole is basically a region of space

18
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,720
where gravity has become so extreme

19
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,400
that once you cross a certain boundary,

20
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,620
you just can't get back out.

21
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:51,720
And I mean anything.

22
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:54,680
You could have the fastest spaceship imaginable,

23
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,000
and it wouldn't even matter.

24
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,440
Nothing can escape this, not even light.

25
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:02,420
And that boundary is called the event horizon.

26
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,560
Now, here's something that's kind of surprising.

27
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:06,680
The event horizon isn't a wall.

28
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,040
You don't reach it and suddenly hit

29
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:11,180
some giant invisible barrier.

30
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:14,420
If you were falling into a really massive black hole,

31
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:17,000
you could actually cross this event horizon

32
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,420
without noticing some dramatic change

33
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:20,740
at that exact moment.

34
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:22,980
The problem is what happens after,

35
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:26,460
because according to Einstein's theory of general relativity,

36
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:30,100
once you're inside, all possible paths forward

37
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,780
eventually lead deeper into the black hole.

38
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,560
And if you keep following that path,

39
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,340
the equations eventually take you

40
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,860
to something called a singularity.

41
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,300
So what's a singularity?

42
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:43,120
It's basically where our current equations

43
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:44,300
stop making sense.

44
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:46,260
They start producing infinities.

45
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:49,160
And whenever physicists get an infinity like that,

46
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:51,400
they don't usually celebrate and say,

47
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:53,600
well, that's obviously what nature does.

48
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,280
No, they tend to think,

49
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,100
okay, something is missing.

50
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,740
And that's where things get really interesting,

51
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,140
because we have two incredibly successful theories.

52
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:07,660
General relativity tells us how gravity and space-time behave,

53
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:12,160
and quantum mechanics tells us how the very small behaves.

54
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:13,780
But inside a black hole,

55
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:15,780
eventually you get into a situation

56
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:18,340
where you need both theories at the same time,

57
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:20,520
and we still don't have a complete theory

58
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:21,600
that combines them.

59
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,160
So honestly, we don't know what the deepest part

60
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:27,480
of a black hole actually looks like.

61
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,940
So the question then is,

62
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,680
what if that singularity isn't really the end?

63
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:33,320
What if something happens there

64
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:36,260
that our current physics simply doesn't know

65
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:37,740
how to describe yet,

66
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:39,640
or doesn't even know it exists?

67
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:42,300
Maybe quantum gravity prevents everything

68
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,460
from collapsing into an actual infinite point,

69
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:49,620
or maybe the collapse reaches some incredibly extreme state

70
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:52,400
and then transitions into something else.

71
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,240
Because what if it starts expanding again

72
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:56,760
and even ends up creating

73
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,180
a completely new region of space-time,

74
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:01,100
something like a new universe?

75
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:03,300
Now just think about that for a second.

76
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,900
Let's say you're living in some giant parent universe.

77
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:10,300
A massive star collapses and it forms a black hole.

78
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:12,500
From your perspective, you look at it and say,

79
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,560
there's a black hole.

80
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:15,240
But inside that black hole,

81
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:19,140
suppose a new expanding region of space-time forms.

82
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,400
The people living inside that region

83
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:24,200
wouldn't necessarily see a black hole.

84
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:26,880
They'd see galaxies, stars, and planets.

85
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:29,420
These people would see an expanding universe,

86
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,380
and eventually they'd probably ask the same question we ask,

87
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,460
where did all of this come from?

88
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:39,140
And their answer could be what they call the Big Bang.

89
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,580
That's the basic idea I'm trying to draw your attention to.

90
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:44,680
Now, before you get too excited,

91
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:47,300
there's something I really want to make clear.

92
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:49,160
Scientists have not discovered

93
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,800
that our universe is inside a black hole.

94
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,800
Actually, there's no evidence right now that proves that.

95
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:57,140
It's just a speculative idea.

96
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,380
But it's also interesting

97
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:01,700
because it connects to a real problem in physics.

98
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,660
We don't actually know what happens

99
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:06,060
at the deepest point inside a black hole,

100
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:08,620
and that's a pretty big hole in our understanding.

101
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,380
Now let's talk about the Big Bang

102
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:13,900
because there's another thing people often get wrong here.

103
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,360
When you hear Big Bang,

104
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,420
you might imagine the universe as this tiny ball

105
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:21,060
that exploded outward into empty space.

106
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,260
That's not really what happened.

107
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:26,740
There wasn't an empty room called space sitting there

108
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:29,280
while the universe exploded inside it.

109
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:31,120
Space itself was expanding.

110
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,980
You see, the early universe was incredibly hot

111
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:35,320
and incredibly dense,

112
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:38,420
and as space expanded, the universe cooled down.

113
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,460
So if a black hole really could somehow produce a new universe,

114
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,380
we're not talking about a black hole going boom.

115
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,760
No, it's something much stranger.

116
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:49,400
The inside of that black hole

117
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:52,700
would somehow become a new expanding space time.

118
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:53,460
Do you get it?

119
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:55,640
And if you lived inside that space time,

120
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:59,520
you'd experience that expansion as the history of your universe,

121
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,240
which then raises a pretty uncomfortable question.

122
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:04,060
If we're inside a black hole,

123
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,780
where is the event horizon?

124
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,180
Where's the edge?

125
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:10,800
And why don't we see some giant black sphere

126
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:12,220
surrounding the universe?

127
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:15,140
Well, this is where our everyday intuition

128
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:16,240
doesn't really help.

129
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:20,120
Remember, an event horizon isn't a physical wall.

130
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:23,000
It's a boundary in space time

131
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,820
that basically tells you

132
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:28,640
which events can still communicate with the outside

133
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:30,260
and which ones can't.

134
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:33,180
So you wouldn't necessarily look through a telescope

135
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:36,500
and see a big black circle around the universe.

136
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:41,160
There might not be anything visually obvious to see.

137
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:47,460
The boundary here is part of the geometry of space time itself,

138
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:50,960
and that means something really strange could be true.

139
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,880
We could, in principle, be inside such a region

140
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:57,000
without being able to look around

141
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,360
and recognize it as a black hole.

142
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:01,280
Now, here's an analogy.

143
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:02,720
Let's imagine a creature

144
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,620
that can only move across a flat sheet of paper.

145
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:07,240
It can go left and right,

146
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:09,860
and it can move forward and backward,

147
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:12,620
but it has no concept of up.

148
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:16,300
Now, imagine you, standing above the paper,

149
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:19,040
pick that creature up and move it somewhere else.

150
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,400
From the creature's perspective,

151
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:23,840
you just did something completely impossible.

152
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:25,960
Unbeknownst to the creature,

153
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:29,220
you didn't break any of the rules of its world.

154
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:33,600
You simply used a direction that it couldn't access.

155
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:36,660
That's roughly the kind of intuition we need

156
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:38,920
when thinking about higher dimensional

157
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,800
or larger space-time structures.

158
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:46,740
I'm not saying our universe is literally a sheet of paper.

159
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:49,660
It's just a way to understand why.

160
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:52,200
Something can look completely impossible

161
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:56,300
from one perspective and perfectly normal from another.

162
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,740
And now we get to the question

163
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,860
that really makes this idea uncomfortable.

164
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:05,180
What's outside our universe?

165
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:09,040
Normally, that question doesn't even make sense, right?

166
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:11,580
If the universe is everything that exists,

167
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:12,920
then what's outside it?

168
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:13,880
Nothing.

169
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:19,280
But suppose our universe is actually the inside of a black hole.

170
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:24,520
Then maybe there is a larger space-time outside of ours,

171
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:26,960
a kind of parent universe.

172
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:31,220
And now just imagine someone in that parent universe

173
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:33,440
looking at our black hole.

174
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:35,680
They might see an event horizon

175
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:38,020
and might even measure its mass.

176
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:41,080
They might watch matter orbiting around it

177
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:41,940
and they'd say,

178
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:42,920
that's a black hole.

179
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:44,600
Meanwhile, inside,

180
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:46,360
we're looking around and saying,

181
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:48,300
this is the universe.

182
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:50,120
It's the same structure,

183
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,560
just two completely different perspectives.

184
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,800
And that's the part I find really fascinating

185
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:00,060
because maybe the word universe

186
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:04,900
doesn't necessarily mean the absolute biggest thing that exists.

187
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:08,300
Maybe it just means the region of space-time

188
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:09,800
we're able to experience.

189
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:14,720
Now, could we ever prove that we're inside a black hole?

190
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:16,900
That's where things get difficult.

191
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:18,320
The thing is,

192
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:22,940
if the outside is genuinely beyond our causal horizon,

193
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:27,200
we can't just build a bigger telescope and look out.

194
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:30,440
It wouldn't matter how powerful the telescope was.

195
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,820
And this is because if information can't reach us from outside,

196
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:37,340
there's absolutely nothing for the telescope to see.

197
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,520
So we'd have to look for indirect evidence.

198
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:45,240
Maybe the way our universe expanded contains some clues,

199
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:47,280
or there could be particular patterns

200
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:49,320
in the cosmic microwave background.

201
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:50,020
Who knows?

202
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:51,880
Maybe the geometry of our universe

203
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:54,300
would have some unusual features

204
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:57,160
or a future theory of quantum gravity

205
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:00,900
would make a very specific prediction that we could test.

206
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:03,900
That's how we'd have to approach it scientifically.

207
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:08,180
We may get to a point where we'd need a prediction that says,

208
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:10,220
if we're inside a black hole,

209
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:12,780
we should observe this or that.

210
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:14,120
And if we don't see it,

211
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:15,380
the idea gets weaker.

212
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:18,380
That's the difference between an interesting thought experiment

213
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:20,240
and a scientific theory.

214
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:21,460
But right now,

215
00:09:21,980 --> 00:09:23,540
we don't have the smoking gun

216
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:27,760
and black holes already give physicists another headache.

217
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:28,860
Information.

218
00:09:29,300 --> 00:09:31,720
This is one of the deepest problems in modern physics.

219
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:35,520
I want you to think of throwing a book into a black hole.

220
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,800
This book contains information.

221
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:44,580
I mean, every word, letter, molecule, everything about its physical state.

222
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:48,700
Quantum mechanics tells us that information is incredibly important.

223
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:51,620
It shouldn't simply disappear from reality.

224
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:55,060
But according to the classical picture of a black hole,

225
00:09:55,320 --> 00:10:00,200
that book falls past the event horizon and eventually reaches the singularity.

226
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:02,400
So what happens to all that information?

227
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:05,800
Then Stephen Hawking made the problem even worse.

228
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:09,460
He discovered that black holes should actually emit radiation.

229
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:11,900
It's called Hawking radiation.

230
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:14,560
So a black hole isn't completely black.

231
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:19,440
It can slowly lose energy and over an unbelievably long period of time,

232
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:21,560
it could eventually evaporate.

233
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:22,140
Okay.

234
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,920
So now imagine the black hole disappears.

235
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:27,320
What happened to the information in the book?

236
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:28,640
Where did it go?

237
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:32,240
Did it somehow come back out through the Hawking radiation?

238
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:37,080
Was it encoded in the radiation in some incredibly complicated way?

239
00:10:37,260 --> 00:10:40,000
Or is there something about our understanding of space-time

240
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,680
that we're fundamentally missing?

241
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:44,600
Physicists have been wrestling with this for decades.

242
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:46,960
And this is why black holes are so important.

243
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:50,140
They're not just weird objects that eat stars.

244
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:54,280
They're basically nature's way of forcing our best theories to collide

245
00:10:54,280 --> 00:11:00,140
from gravity to quantum mechanics, information, space, time, just name them.

246
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,400
And right now we don't have the complete answer,

247
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:05,960
which brings us back to our original question.

248
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:09,300
What if our universe is inside a black hole?

249
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:10,820
Maybe, maybe not.

250
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:11,740
We don't even know.

251
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:15,580
And I think that's actually more interesting than pretending we have an answer

252
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:20,600
because if it were true, the Big Bang might not have been the beginning of everything.

253
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:23,200
Or it might have been the beginning of our universe.

254
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:26,620
There could be something before it or something outside it.

255
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:28,640
Maybe even another universe.

256
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:31,940
And here's where you can take the idea one step further.

257
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:35,220
What if black holes don't just create one universe?

258
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:36,920
What if they can create many?

259
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:38,960
I mean, I'm just thinking out loud.

260
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:40,420
Just imagine a universe.

261
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:45,500
Stars form and some eventually die or collapse into black holes.

262
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:48,040
And if these speculative models were correct,

263
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:51,780
perhaps some of those black holes end up producing new universes.

264
00:11:52,180 --> 00:11:54,120
And those universes form stars.

265
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:57,180
Those stars then form more black holes.

266
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,840
And those black holes potentially produce even more universes.

267
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:04,320
And you'd have this huge cosmic family tree

268
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:08,500
where a universe could have a parent and that parent could have a parent.

269
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:10,760
And maybe there was something before that.

270
00:12:11,020 --> 00:12:16,700
Now, again, we have no evidence that this cosmic family tree actually exists.

271
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:18,900
This isn't established science.

272
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:21,560
But it raises a really beautiful question.

273
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:24,860
Maybe the universe isn't a single isolated thing.

274
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:25,820
Who knows?

275
00:12:26,100 --> 00:12:29,860
Maybe reality is much bigger than the part we're able to experience.

276
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,920
And maybe what we call the beginning of the universe

277
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:37,380
is actually just the beginning of our particular corner of reality.

278
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:39,720
So are we inside a black hole?

279
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,760
Right now, there's no way for me to honestly tell you yes.

